{"language_code":"eng","language_probability":1.0,"text":"In 1731, a British captain claimed Spanish sailors cut off his ear, and that ear gave a whole war its name. His name was Robert Jenkins, and his ship, the Rebecca, was stopped near Cuba by a Spanish coast guard hunting for smugglers. Jenkins said they tied him up, and the Spanish captain sliced off his ear with a sword. And he said the captain told him, \"Go tell your king I'll do the same to him.\" Legend says Jenkins kept the ear in a jar and carried it home to England. In 1738, he was questioned by Parliament, and legend says he even held up the ear, but historians doubt that. Still, the press and the politicians screamed for revenge. The prime minister, Robert Walpole, did not want a war. But in 1739, Britain declared war on Spain. [laughs] The name came much later, in 1858, from the writer Thomas Carlyle, the War of Jenkins' Ear. In 1741, Admiral Vernon attacked the Spanish city of Cartagena with about 180 ships, but Spain had Blas de Lezo with only six ships and about 3,000 men. A tough admiral with one eye, one arm, and one leg, and Spain won. Thousands of British soldiers died, most of them from disease. [laughs] Even funnier, legend says Vernon had victory medals made before the battle was won. The war merged into an even bigger war that lasted until 1748, and historians say the ear was just an excuse. The real fight was about trade. So if you like history where the truth is stranger than the legend, follow for more stories like this.","words":[{"text":"In","start":0.0,"end":0.18,"type":"word","logprob":-0.0013259199913591146},{"text":" ","start":0.18,"end":0.22,"type":"spacing","logprob":-3.2186455882765586e-6},{"text":"1731,","start":0.22,"end":1.32,"type":"word","logprob":-0.000030181740822854408},{"text":" ","start":1.32,"end":1.52,"type":"spacing","logprob":-1.9073468138230965e-6},{"text":"a","start":1.52,"end":1.62,"type":"word","logprob":-1.9073468138230965e-6},{"text":" ","start":1.62,"end":1